WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.483
- Nature, the most powerful
creative force on Earth.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.400
I'm Chef Melissa King.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.830
Cooking has taken me to incredible places.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:14.260
Magical. (laughs)

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.080
From TV competitions and celebrity galas

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.580
to countries around the world,

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:22.360
I'm heading out to places
I've never been before

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.100
to seek out new experiences

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.370
and to create new dishes
inspired by nature.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.530
Wow, this is amazing.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:32.200
I'm teaming up with some of
the world's greatest explorers

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.360
to go further than I've ever gone before.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.470
This is insane.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:36.303
- Stunning.

00:00:36.303 --> 00:00:39.343
- To push my craft,
creativity, and my cooking.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.777
This is "Tasting Wild."

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:50.860
(upbeat music)

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.980
We are here just outside
of Port Angeles, Washington

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.503
on the northern coast of
the Olympic Peninsula.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.223
This place is a really picturesque area.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.560
There's an incredible forest

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.143
that hits this dramatic ocean coastline.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.840
I'm here to meet on Anand Varma,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.170
who shoots incredible photos of nature.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.750
His photography and work
really shows details

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.313
that I've never seen before.

00:01:17.166 --> 00:01:19.480
(birds chirping)

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.610
I'm meeting him at an apiary,

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:25.033
a bee farm that I heard produces
an extremely unique honey.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:27.393
- [Anand] Hello.

00:01:27.393 --> 00:01:28.450
- Hey, Anand.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:29.620
- So nice to meet you, Melissa.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:30.453
- How are you?

00:01:30.453 --> 00:01:31.750
- Doing great, doing great.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:34.360
Have you ever worn a bee suit before?

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.417
- I have not.

00:01:35.417 --> 00:01:36.460
- All right.
- It's a first.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:37.610
- All right.
- I always wanted to do this.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:38.950
- Yeah, this is gonna be your suit.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:39.970
Let's get suited up-
- Thank you.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:41.350
- And check out what's going on here.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:42.183
- Cool.

00:01:42.183 --> 00:01:43.540
- One of the early stories I worked on

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.310
for National Geographic was on honeybees,

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.070
and I actually kept a
beehive in my backyard.

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:50.890
And that let me kind of study their lives.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.320
You get up close, and you take
the time to pay attention,

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.240
and you realize how much beauty

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.390
and complexity is all around
us everywhere you look.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:00.730
Have you ever looked
inside a beehive before?

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.560
- No, first time.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.300
I've been to apiaries,

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.030
but first time being able to get up close.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.460
I'm curious of the flavor profile.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:10.600
I love honey.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.430
I cook with honey all the time.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.130
But to be able to like
get up close and personal,

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:16.510
I'm excited.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.040
Can't wait to taste this honey.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.100
- [Anand] I've heard
great things about this.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.977
- Yeah?
- I haven't tried it yet.

00:02:20.977 --> 00:02:22.123
- Hi.
- Hi, hi.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:25.480
Hello, this is Dan and Judy Harvey.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.823
We've been here since 1985.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:31.810
And when you have healthy bees

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.170
in the right weather conditions,
they make a lot of honey.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.330
- [Judy] Are you ready for this, Melissa?

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.820
- [Melissa] Yeah, I'm super excited.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:40.990
- So one of the really cool things

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:44.350
about this area is this
native plant called fireweed.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.500
And so what Dan and Judy have
done is set up their hives

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.900
so that the bees are almost exclusively

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.270
collecting the nectar and pollen

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:53.740
from this one kind of plant.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.630
This native plant,
fireweed, doesn't have a lot

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:57.640
of pesticides or herbicides sprayed on it.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.890
So that means that the
honey is super clean.

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:02.470
It's super pure, and it's all
coming from a single flower.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.110
So it has a unique flavor profile.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.670
- Wow, that's what I'm curious about,

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:09.490
is the flavor notes of the fireweed

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:12.100
and what makes it unique and special.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.420
- I can tell from Melissa's work,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.660
she's somebody who pays really
close attention to her craft.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.390
And so as somebody who's that tuned in,

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:20.770
I'm really curious what kinds

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.110
of things she'll pick up on here.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:24.850
- So if you'd like to see a queen,

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.770
this is gonna be the
quickest way to see it.

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.420
- [Melissa] The king
needs to meet her queen.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.730
- [Judy] Aw. (laughs)

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.070
- [Melissa] Each box is a different queen?

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.080
- [Dan] Each box is a different queen.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.460
You can see the pollen.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.544
That's all pollen right there.

00:03:38.544 --> 00:03:39.377
- Yeah.
- Oh, yeah.

00:03:39.377 --> 00:03:41.260
- [Dan] This is the honey, the nectar.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.210
- [Anand] And the pollen's all
one color, and you can tell

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:44.590
that there's this-
- Yeah, mm-hm.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.300
- [Anand] Can you flip it
over and just set it here?

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.133
I'd love to take a picture

00:03:47.133 --> 00:03:49.093
of some of these bees.
- Oh, that's a great idea.

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.840
- When I decided to become a photographer,

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.330
macro photography became my specialty.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:59.200
Nature is kind of the ultimate
inspiration to keep exploring

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.540
and to keep discovering new details.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.360
It's so surprising when you
get up close to a honeybee,

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.540
especially its face, you
realize they're covered in fur.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.400
There's hairs coming
out of their eyeballs.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.800
You learn to appreciate
them in a whole new way.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.700
- Honey comes in a wide range of flavors.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.510
You can really use it from
savory dishes to sweet dishes.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:21.070
It's almost like wine.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.690
There's so many different nuances

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:25.273
that you can get from a single honey.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:28.420
This is incredible.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.450
I love seeing all the bees on
here and what they're doing.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:32.590
Let's go taste some honey.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.330
- [Dan] Okay, let's go.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:35.260
- That was so cool.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:36.370
- [Judy] Yeah, loved it.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:38.200
- [Melissa] That was amazing.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.700
- [Judy] I think there's
something really unique

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.800
about fireweed honey.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.870
- Oh wow, mm.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:44.703
Wanna dip my finger in there.

00:04:44.703 --> 00:04:46.630
- It's so good.
- Yeah.

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:50.440
It's very light, but
also very clean-tasting.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.993
It's so delicate in flavor profile.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:56.830
- We thank you for coming.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:58.090
It's been a pleasure.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:04:59.830
It's always exciting to have somebody come

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:02.650
who's interested enough to
wanna know more details.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.390
Well, we'll see you hopefully again.

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:05.380
- All right, thanks so much.
- Thank you.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:06.213
- [Judy] Yeah.

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:10.250
- [Melissa] That bee farm was incredible.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.850
- Yeah, I think what they're
doing is really cool.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:14.200
- Leaving the apiary,

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:17.290
Anand tells me we're going
to investigate a tide pool.

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:19.450
It's one of my favorite things to do.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:21.790
I've tide-pooled around
the coast of California

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:24.940
and up through Oregon,
but never this far north.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:26.440
I love seafood so much.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:29.230
It's one of my favorite
ingredients to work with,

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:32.410
and I'm just excited to explore this area

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.720
and see if there's mussels or barnacles,

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.030
maybe even clams that we can find.

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:39.370
- I can't wait to show
you what's in there.

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:40.570
There's this island.

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:42.340
It's surrounded by tide pools,

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.100
tons of cool little stuff in those pools.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:46.420
- I've seen some of your work,

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:51.220
and I've seen beads of water
spraying off of a hummingbird,

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.570
which is just incredible

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.973
to be able to capture that type of detail.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:59.080
So what got you into macro photography?

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.420
- I think it was just what I did as a kid.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:03.550
I would head out the back door

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:06.250
in my childhood home in Atlanta,

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:08.200
and I'd head down to the
creek in my backyard.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:12.190
And it was like every
time you pull up a rock

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:13.810
or turn over a log,

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:18.040
there'd be some weird little
bug or snake or salamander.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:19.240
Then somewhere along the way,

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.790
I fell in love with
exploring the natural world,

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:25.120
and I found out that
photography could be this way

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:28.060
of sharing my discoveries
with other people.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:29.890
Every time I've ever been tide-pooling,

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:32.320
I see a creature I've
never seen in my life.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:35.200
That's one of the rewards
of paying close attention.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.720
You spend the time to look
at something carefully,

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:39.280
and you get to discover something new,

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.773
and it motivates you to keep going.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.650
So we are in a pretty special spot

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:46.990
where the tide has pulled out,

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:49.600
and it's gonna allow us
to explore a lot more

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:53.230
than what we'd be able to
see any other time of day.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.630
- I'm hoping to really
tie in all the ingredients

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:59.380
that we find in this
tide pool into a dish.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:00.460
This is insane.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:01.935
- Stunning.
- Yeah.

00:07:01.935 --> 00:07:03.400
- All right, so I think
we gotta pick our way down

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.923
this kind of cliff here.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.820
- We're making our way down these rocks,

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:13.720
and I see mussels, barnacles, starfish.

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:16.540
Wow, look at all the seaweed.
- Yeah.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:18.970
- [Melissa] Look at this, kombu.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:20.830
- [Anand] Have you ever
harvested your own?

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:21.820
- Absolutely.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:23.500
Yeah, you can dry all this

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.690
and use it for any sort of applications

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:27.335
from broths to soups.

00:07:27.335 --> 00:07:28.750
- Oh, cool.

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:31.540
- [Melissa] There's so
much life here to explore.

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:34.030
- [Anand] You see a
rock covered in mussels,

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:35.830
and then you get down
to one of those mussels,

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.120
and it's covered in limpets.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:39.460
And that limpet has its own algae,

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:43.960
and there's just these
levels upon levels of detail

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:45.310
and life and biodiversity.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:48.542
And that's what makes
tide pools so special.

00:07:48.542 --> 00:07:51.368
- Wow, this is so cool.

00:07:51.368 --> 00:07:52.750
- Whoa.
- Right, look.

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:53.583
- What is that?

00:07:53.583 --> 00:07:54.760
- They're just peeking out.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:55.750
- [Anand] Look at how much it's moving.

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:57.150
- [Melissa] Like a dinosaur.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.533
- That's wild.

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:03.670
You can kind of see there's a big rock

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:04.503
in the cave.
- Oh yeah, right over there.

00:08:04.503 --> 00:08:06.770
- If we aim for the left side of that,

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:07.687
I think that's gonna be-

00:08:07.687 --> 00:08:08.732
- Tide's coming in already.

00:08:08.732 --> 00:08:10.960
- Yeah.
- It was fast.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.340
- [Anand] We start turning rocks here.

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:14.050
I think there's some cool stuff to find.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:14.890
- [Melissa] Yeah?

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:18.883
It's like a starfish, like
a baby, baby starfish.

00:08:21.172 --> 00:08:23.950
Oh, wow.
- Oh my God.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:25.453
I have never seen this before.

00:08:25.453 --> 00:08:27.490
- What is that?

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:28.660
Wow.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:31.660
There's so many colors and
creatures here to explore,

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:32.980
and it's really endless.

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:34.870
I'm feeling inspired by all the layers

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:36.310
that are being revealed.

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:37.900
It makes me think about my own cooking

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:39.520
and the layers within food.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.345
- [Anand] Look at this.

00:08:41.345 --> 00:08:42.310
It's a sea star.

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:43.390
- [Melissa] What?

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:45.100
- They call these flood stars.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:47.110
- No way.

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:48.560
- [Anand] Look at that color.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:52.750
- The tide's coming in,

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:55.453
but I wanna go look for
some clams on the beach.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:01.270
I can't believe we were just out there.

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:02.350
- It's surrounded by water now.

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:05.320
It's like you've gotta take a
boat to get to where we were.

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:06.370
What are we looking for?
- All right.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:08.800
So you just kind of start raking.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:10.660
When you see little bubbles like that,

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:11.890
there might be something there.

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:13.270
Let's see what happens.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:15.760
So you'll find cockles, littlenecks.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:16.593
- Oh, whoa.

00:09:16.593 --> 00:09:17.620
What's that? What's that?

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:18.873
- We've got littleneck.
- Oh cool.

00:09:19.874 --> 00:09:20.707
All right.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:23.903
- Got some littlenecks,

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:25.653
some cockles.
- Little baby cockle.

00:09:25.653 --> 00:09:27.460
- It's a little baby cockle.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:28.513
It's gonna be tasty.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:31.637
But yeah, these are gonna
be great for steaming.

00:09:31.637 --> 00:09:33.040
- Okay.
- The littlenecks.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.830
I think we'll do some sort of tom kha

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:38.200
or some sort of steamed
clam and mussel dish,

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:39.940
I think could be fun.
- It sounds amazing.

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:41.290
- [Melissa] As we're
heading back to the car,

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:43.690
we find some beautiful-looking sea beans,

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:46.040
which I'd love to include
in my dishes somehow.

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:48.430
Give it a try.

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:50.053
Kind tastes like the ocean.

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:52.570
- Oh, wow.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:55.120
It's like a salty pickle almost.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:56.070
- Exactly.
- Yeah.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:00.010
- [Melissa] The details
we saw today at the apiary

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.440
and along the coast were astonishing.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:04.630
I can't wait to integrate
all the flavors we found

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:06.403
into a meal for Anand tomorrow.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.107
Okay, we're almost there.

00:10:11.107 --> 00:10:13.090
I'm so excited for you to see this spot

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:14.500
on the coast that I've found.
- Yeah?

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:15.430
Awesome.

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:17.230
- Yeah, it's really beautiful.

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:19.150
It's gonna be great to be
able to cook out on the beach.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:23.080
We're gonna set up some live
fire, do a little cookout.

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:24.340
- So while you're setting up,

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.560
I'd love to create a little field studio,

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:28.000
and I'll shoot some of the ingredients.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:31.030
I'll get some closeups of
the mussels in particular.

